@page "/toast"

<CatHead Title="Toasts" Tier="@CatHead.Tier3">
    A confirmation that appears, says what happened, and goes away. Anything the reader still has to
    act on is an <a href="/alert">alert</a>, which stays until the state changes.
</CatHead>

<CatGroup Title="When to use a toast">
    <h3 id="when-not">When not to use one <CatAnchor Id="when-not" /></h3>
    <div class="callout callout--warn">
        <i class="ri-error-warning-line"></i>
        <div class="callout-body">
            <span class="callout-title">A toast can be missed, so nothing important may live only in one</span>
            A toast appears away from where the reader is looking and goes again on its own. A screen
            reader announces it once, politely, and may not announce it at all while something else is
            being read; a reader using a screen magnifier is usually looking at another part of the
            screen and never sees it. So:
            <ul>
                <li>Information the reader needs, or something that stays true &mdash; read-only mode, a
                    failed connection, a validation error &mdash; goes <strong>inline</strong>, beside what
                    it is about, or in an <a href="/alert">alert</a> banner that stays until the state
                    changes.</li>
                <li>Something the reader must act on goes in the page or in a <a href="/modal">dialog</a>.
                    A toast can carry an action as a shortcut, never as the only way to it.</li>
                <li>Something the reader should be able to come back to goes in the
                    <a href="/notifications">notification inbox</a>; a toast may announce it as it
                    arrives.</li>
            </ul>
            A toast is for a receipt: confirmation of something the reader just did, that would change
            nothing if they missed it.
        </div>
    </div>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.ToastOrAlert" Title="Toast or alert" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            The same event, told both ways. A toast is a receipt for something that already happened and
            needs nothing from the reader; an <a href="/alert">alert</a> stays until the state changes,
            and is where an action the reader <em>must</em> take belongs. A notification card can offer
            one, but a toast can be dismissed, so nothing that has to happen may depend on it.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Raising a toast">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.ToastTriggers" Title="Tones and timeout">
        <p>
            <strong>Live.</strong> <code>sednaUi.toast(message, options)</code> renders the stack and the
            toast, stacks a second under the first, and removes it after its timeout;
            <code>timeout: 0</code> keeps it until dismissed, which is what a failure wants. From C# it
            is <code>ToastAsync</code>. <code>kind</code> is <code>go</code>, <code>warn</code>,
            <code>danger</code> or <code>info</code>, and <code>info</code> is the default. Each button
            here takes the colour of what it does, not of the toast it raises.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.MoreTones" Title="Brand and secret tones" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>Beside go, warn, danger and info, a toast takes <code>brand</code> and <code>secret</code>, from script as <code>kind</code> too.</p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.Pile" Title="Several at once" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            Several toasts at once can take over a corner of the screen. Pile them, cap how many show, and
            let a pointer swipe one away. All three are the library's stack; nothing changes in what you
            write.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Live.</strong> <code>sednaUi.configure({ toastPile: true })</code> &mdash;
            <code>SednaUiOptions.ToastPile</code> from C# &mdash; gives the stack
            <code>.toast-stack--pile</code>: the newest toast in front and the two before it peeking out
            above. The pointer or the focus on the stack opens it out, and while it is open no toast in
            it runs out. <code>toastLimit: 3</code> (<code>ToastLimit</code>) shows the newest three;
            older ones wait out of sight with their timers held and come back as the ones in front go.
            A stack you write takes <code>.toast-stack--pile</code> too and opens the same way.
        </p>
        <p>
            A toast with a close button can be swiped toward the edge the stack sits on &mdash; the
            inline end, so leftward in a right-to-left page; a short swipe springs back. The close
            button stays the way to do it without a pointer. With reduced motion the pile opens and a
            toast springs back in one step.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Work that ends later">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.ToastHandleFromCsharp" Title="Upload from C#" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live — upload it.</strong> <code>ToastAsync</code> returns a
            <code>SednaToast</code>: show <em>Uploading…</em> with <code>timeoutMs: 0</code>, and when the
            work ends <code>ReplaceAsync</code> the outcome into the same toast, in place. The button is
            <code>aria-busy</code> while the work runs, and a failure is written on the page beside it
            as well, where it stays until an upload succeeds.
            <code>DismissAsync</code> removes it early. A toast the reader already closed is not an error:
            replacing it shows a new one, because the outcome still has to reach them. The close button's
            name is <code>SednaUiOptions.ToastDismissLabel</code> — the one word the library writes
            itself — or <code>dismissLabel</code> for one toast.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.PromiseToast" Title="Save from script" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live.</strong> Work that ends later is one toast, not two: present a toast for the
            running work from your own <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> &mdash; a <code>.spinner</code>
            where the icon goes, no close button, <code>timeout: 0</code> &mdash; and when the promise
            settles, <code>sednaUi.toast.replace(id, message, options)</code> turns it into the outcome
            in place. A failure the reader has to act on is never only in the toast: show it on the page
            too, beside the work, as an <a href="/alert">alert</a> that stays until a save succeeds. The
            alert sits in a wrapper carrying <code>hidden</code>, because <code>.alert</code>&rsquo;s own
            display outranks the attribute. From C#, <code>PresentToastAsync</code> then
            <code>ReplaceAsync</code> on the handle it returns does the same.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Toasts you write">
    <p class="cat-lede">
        A notification that arrives while the reader is elsewhere &mdash; a task assigned, a value to
        check &mdash; is a toast with something to author: a body that opens the record, buttons of its
        own. Write it once in a <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> and present it in the library's stack, so it
        has the top layer above an open modal, the hold on its timer and one corner shared with every
        other toast. Never position a second stack of your own: it collides with this one and loses all
        three.
    </p>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.NotificationCard" Title="Notification card" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live.</strong> <code>sednaUi.toast.present(templateId, data, options)</code> clones
            the template's first element into the stack and fills its <code>data-*</code> slots with text
            from <code>data</code> &mdash; <code>title</code> fills <code>[data-title]</code> &mdash; and
            <code>href</code> becomes the <code>href</code> of its <code>[data-href]</code> link. A body
            that is the thing to click is an <code>a.toast-body</code> or a
            <code>button.toast-body</code>, so <kbd>Tab</kbd> reaches it apart from the close button;
            <code>.toast-actions</code> holds buttons under the text. A control with
            <code>data-toast-value</code> ends the toast with that value, which
            <code>sednaUi.toast.result(id)</code> resolves with; a <code>.toast-close</code> ends it with
            <code>null</code>, as a dismissal or the timeout do. A link still navigates.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.NotificationsFromCsharp" Title="Cards from C#, with +N more" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; press it five times.</strong> <code>PresentToastAsync</code> returns a
            <code>SednaToast</code>, and <code>ResultAsync</code> waits for what the reader did with it
            &mdash; <code>"open"</code>, <code>"read"</code>, or <code>null</code> &mdash; for as long as
            that takes, the way <code>ShowModalAsync</code> waits for a dialog. Three cards show at once;
            the rest fold into one card whose count <code>ReplaceFromTemplateAsync</code> moves in place,
            keeping its position and the wait already on it. A template that is not there answers
            <code>null</code> rather than throwing into the circuit.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Markup and API">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Toast.Toast" Title="Stack markup" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            You do not write this for <code>sednaUi.toast</code>, and a toast you write goes in a
            <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> for <code>toast.present</code>. It is here for an app that
            renders a stack inside its own page, such as a server-rendered one. <strong>The stack carries the live region, and on this path it is yours to
            write:</strong> <code>role="status"</code> plus <code>aria-live="polite"</code> on
            <code>.toast-stack</code>, or a toast an app rendered is never announced at all. Do not put
            <code>role="alert"</code> on an individual toast — two live regions announce the same text
            twice, and a failure that has to interrupt gets <code>aria-live="assertive"</code> on the
            stack while it is showing.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatSnippet Name="Toast/ToastApi.txt" Title="Script API">
        <p>
            The JavaScript call returns a function that removes that toast early, whose
            <code>id</code> also names it for <code>toast.dismiss(id)</code> and
            <code>toast.replace(id, message, options)</code>. <code>toast.present</code> and
            <code>toast.result</code> are a toast you write and how it ended. <code>dismissLabel</code> names the close
            button, and <code>configure({ toastDismissLabel })</code> sets it once for every toast;
            <code>configure({ toastPile, toastLimit })</code> sets the stack's shape.
        </p>
    </CatSnippet>
</CatGroup>
